{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2487 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "336419abf5d7d09fcbc8bea761447ec1b8f09c2b07dd30a923553848ebfd351a" :edb/db-before-t 2486 :edb/db-after-t 2487 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535799 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:28:07.257Z" 13194139535799 true] [17592186124323 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535799 false] [17592186124323 1100 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186124323 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= r\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2r\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (r\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (2r\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  + 2r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 false] [17592186124323 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186124324 1100 "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535799 false] [17592186124324 1100 "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186124324 1145 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186124324 1145 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial r} = \\cos 3\\theta , \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } =-3r\\sin 3\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= (\\cos 3\\theta )\\cos \\theta  - \\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\sin 3\\theta )\\sin \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\cos \\theta \\cos 3\\theta  + 3\\sin \\theta \\sin 3\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 false] [17592186124325 1100 "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2)$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 false] [17592186124325 1100 "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186124325 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta$, and $z$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand $z=z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = 2xz^{3}, \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} = 4yz^{3}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta$, and $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= 2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} &= 4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-2r^{2}z^{3}\\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta  + 4r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= 2r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}z^{3}\\sin 2\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 false] [17592186124325 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186124515 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366754 13194139535799 true] [17592186124515 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366761 13194139535799 true] [17592186124515 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366768 13194139535799 true] [17592186124515 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366775 13194139535799 true] [17592186124515 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366782 13194139535799 true] [17592186124516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366747 13194139535799 true] [17592186124516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366789 13194139535799 true] [17592186124516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366796 13194139535799 true] [17592186124516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366803 13194139535799 true] [17592186124516 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366810 13194139535799 true] [17592186124517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366817 13194139535799 true] [17592186124517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366824 13194139535799 true] [17592186124517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366831 13194139535799 true] [17592186125159 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535799 false] [17592186125159 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535799 true] [17592186125159 1100 "Given that $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2yz$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta }$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 false] [17592186125159 1100 "Given that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186125159 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2z)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186125159 1145 "In cylindrical coordinates, $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$, so $$f=r^2+2rz\\sin\\theta.$$ Hold $r$ and $z$ fixed while differentiating with respect to $\\theta$: $$f_\\theta=2rz\\cos\\theta.$$" 13194139535799 false] [17592186125164 1100 "Given that $f(x, y, z) = xy + y^{2}z + xz^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 false] [17592186125164 1100 "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy+{y}^{2}z+x{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186125164 1145 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186125164 1145 "Substitution gives $$f=r^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta\\,z+r\\cos\\theta\\,z^2.$$ Holding $r$ and $\\theta$ fixed, differentiate each term with respect to $z$ to get $$f_z=r^2\\sin^2\\theta+2rz\\cos\\theta.$$" 13194139535799 false] [17592186366747 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366748 13194139535799 true] [17592186366747 1089 "q-165282" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366747 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535799 true] [17592186366747 1100 "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." 13194139535799 true] [17592186366747 1113 #uuid "88fe0e29-ee8f-57ea-b3f1-0e9eed66bee2" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366747 1145 "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366749 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366750 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366751 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366752 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366753 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1041 "selection" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366749 13194139535799 true] [17592186366748 1110 #uuid "9f65549c-eab0-5032-9eca-43a375b5fa09" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366749 1034 "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139535799 true] [17592186366749 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535799 true] [17592186366749 1111 #uuid "7c06393f-3c6e-550b-ae5d-be0bc7099075" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366750 1034 "r(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" 13194139535799 true] [17592186366750